I am using ggplot to create maps for visualizing the output of a predictive model for spatial data. I would like to visualize the mean and variance (error) of the data simultaneously. One method for doing this would be to use an HSL model for my scale_color_gradientn() where hue is mapped to the mean and either saturation or luminance is mapped to variance. Is it possible to map the aspects of a color to different variables in ggplot?

For the sake of a minimal working example, I've borrowed from the documentation for scale_colour_gradientn().

library(ggplot2)
library(RColorBrewer)
dsub <- subset <http://www.inside-r.org/r-doc/base/subset>(diamonds, x > 5 & x < 6 & y > 5 & y < 6)
dsub <- mutate(dsub, var=x, diff=sqrt <http://www.inside-r.org/r-doc/base/MathFun>(abs <http://www.inside-r.org/r-doc/base/MathFun>(x-y))* sign <http://www.inside-r.org/r-doc/base/sign>(x-y))
d <- qplot <http://docs.ggplot2.org/0.9.2.1/qplot.html>(x, y, data=dsub, colour=diff)
d + scale_color_gradientn(colours=brewer.pal(7,"YlOrRd"))

Note that there is now a variable "var" in dsub, which I'd like to map to luminance or saturation, or otherwise visualize simultaneously with diff.

Cheers,
Gregory

